2. Synchronized Blocks

Idea: Instead of locking the whole method, you can lock only a small part of the code - the one that really needs protection.

Real-Life Example:

Let's say students are posting announcements on a college notice board. Only one student can write at a time. But others can read the board or do other things while someone writes.

Java Code:
class msg
{
	public void show(String name)
	{                                  // Home
		;;;;;; // 100 LOC
		synchronized(this)
		{
			for(int i=1;i <= 3;i++)
			{
				System.out.println("How are U "+name);
			}
		}
		;;;;;; // 100 LOC
		
	}
	
}

class OurThread extends Thread
{
	msg m;
	String name;
	OurThread(msg m, String name)
	{
		this.m=m;
		this.name=name;
	}
	public void run()
	{
		m.show(name);
	}
}

class SynBlock
{
	public static void main(String[] args) 
	{
		msg  m = new msg(); // m has a lock
		OurThread t1=new OurThread(m,"akhilesh");
		OurThread t2=new OurThread(m,"ankush");

		t2. start();
	}	
}
